Architecture plan for Issue #158: Revalidate public pages immediately after admin post mutations

Current architecture
- Frontend: Next.js App Router under app/, with public pages in app/(public) and admin pages in app/admin.
- Backend: Rust Axum API under rust-backend/, with public post APIs in rust-backend/src/posts/mod.rs and admin mutations in rust-backend/src/posts/admin.rs.
- Frontend data access: Next server components read public/admin data through lib/api/rust-blog.ts and lib/admin/cms-api.ts.
- Deployment topology: reverse proxy routes /api/* to the Rust backend and all other requests to Next.js. The requested revalidation endpoint is the intentional exception: a Next route handler should live under app/api/revalidate/route.ts and must remain reachable by the Rust backend. If reverse proxy precedence would send all /api/* traffic to Rust, either route this specific path to Next or use an internal Next origin for the Rust callback.

Planned module/file organization
- Add app/api/revalidate/route.ts:
  - Accept POST JSON containing at least slug and optionally category/listing hints.
  - Guard with a shared secret from env, e.g. REVALIDATE_SECRET, supplied via Authorization: Bearer <secret> or a secret header.
  - Call revalidatePath("/") for the home/listing page.
  - Call revalidatePath(`/blog/${slug}`) for concrete article paths when slug is present.
  - Call revalidatePath for category/listing routes currently present under app/(public)/blog/category/[category] and paginated list routes as needed.
  - Return small JSON status and avoid leaking the secret.
- Add/extend Rust config in rust-backend/src/config.rs:
  - Read optional/required revalidation settings such as NEXT_REVALIDATE_URL or SELF_URL-derived endpoint plus REVALIDATE_SECRET.
  - Keep startup validation clear; if the feature is enabled, require the secret and URL.
- Add Rust revalidation helper module, e.g. rust-backend/src/revalidate.rs:
  - Small async function to POST to the Next route after mutations.
  - Include slug/category/status context where available.
  - Log failures but do not fail the admin mutation after the database/object-storage work has succeeded.
- Update rust-backend/src/posts/admin.rs:
  - After successful create/update/publish/unpublish/delete, trigger the helper with the affected slug and relevant old slug when slug changes or post is deleted.
  - Revalidate list/category pages for every successful mutation because publication state, featured status, category, and ordering can affect public listings.

Dependencies and sequencing
1. Define the Next revalidation API contract and secret format first.
2. Add Rust config/state plumbing for the endpoint/secret.
3. Add the Rust helper and call it from all successful admin mutation paths.
4. Add tests for the route guard/path selection and Rust mutation callback behavior where practical.
5. Update deployment env documentation (.env.example/README) with REVALIDATE_SECRET and any internal Next callback URL if required.

Key design decisions
- Revalidation is best-effort after a successful mutation: user-facing admin saves should not be rolled back if cache invalidation fails; failures should be logged for operations visibility.
- Use concrete paths such as /blog/<slug> in addition to broad listing paths to avoid relying solely on dynamic pattern invalidation.
- Revalidate both old and new article paths when slugs change, preventing stale old-slug pages.
- Keep the secret independent from admin credentials/JWT secrets so it can be rotated separately.
- Preserve the existing two-process architecture: Next owns cache invalidation; Rust owns persistence and triggers invalidation via HTTP.
